<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Read-face data refresh, on a schedule (TCRN-AOS-INC-049; TCRN-CROSS-INC-032).

  WHAT PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. The LAN read face serves a BUILD-TIME snapshot. The
  chains move on; the page does not, and nothing about a stale page looks stale —
  it renders perfectly, with last week's answer.

  WHY THIS MACHINE AND NOT THE VM. Ruled 2026-08-04: building the data needs the
  web toolchain; the VM has an engine and no node_modules by design. The trade is
  honest: while this laptop is off, the read face goes stale, and only
  `pnpm read-face:freshness` says so.

  THREE DEFECTS FIXED HERE, all measured rather than reasoned (INC-032):

  1. PATH. The original ran `/bin/sh -lc … pnpm …`. Under launchd a login `sh`
     does NOT source ~/.zshrc, which is what puts the nvm-managed pnpm on PATH,
     so EVERY fire died at the first command with exit 127 — the schedule had
     never once succeeded from the day it was installed, and the only page update
     anyone saw came from a hand-run apply. Reproduce the root cause with:
         env -i /bin/sh -lc 'command -v pnpm'      # prints nothing
     PATH is therefore stated here explicitly. The nvm entry is version-pinned to
     match package.json's `engines.node` (24.16.0); if that pin moves, this moves.

  2. It shipped nothing a browser loads. The original pipeline ran
     `cockpit:data` + `apply-read-face.mjs`, but apply-read-face transfers only
     the read service's runner and unit — the page both faces serve is deployed by
     apply.mjs into /srv/tcrn/aos-cockpit/runtime/web. Every step exited 0 and the
     page stayed stale. apply.mjs projects the data and builds the web in the
     right order internally.

  3. StartInterval was skipped outright whenever the laptop slept through a fire.
     StartCalendarInterval coalesces missed fires into one run at wake.

  4. The closing freshness check raced the deploy it was checking. apply.mjs
     replaces the static root the read face serves (rsync --archive --delete), so a
     probe fired the instant apply returns can hit the swap window and answer
     `not-judged` / exit 2 — measured on the first real run after fix 1, while the
     page it was judging was in fact correct seconds later. The retry below is
     bounded and only tolerates UNREACHABILITY: a genuinely stale page still fails
     all three attempts and the job still exits non-zero. Do not widen it into a
     retry of the freshness predicate itself.

  A schedule that fails is now itself checked: `pnpm scheduler:health` reads the
  last exit status of every com.tcrn.* agent and goes red on a non-zero one.
  Nothing did that before, which is why a 127 could run for a day unseen.

  TEMPLATE, NOT A LOADED AGENT — same rule as the paired-backup plist beside it.
  The path is one machine's; loading is the operator's act, not the repository's.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.tcrn.aos.read-face-refresh</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>export PATH="$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.16.0/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; cd "$HOME/Code/TCRN Platform/TCRN-AOS" &amp;&amp; pnpm build &amp;&amp; node deploy/aos-resident-cockpit/apply.mjs &amp;&amp; node deploy/aos-resident-cockpit/apply-read-face.mjs &amp;&amp; for i in 1 2 3; do sleep 10; pnpm read-face:freshness &amp;&amp; exit 0; done; exit 1</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <array>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>0</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>20</integer></dict>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>4</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>20</integer></dict>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>8</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>20</integer></dict>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>12</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>20</integer></dict>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>16</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>20</integer></dict>
    <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>20</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>20</integer></dict>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/tcrn-aos-read-face-refresh.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/tcrn-aos-read-face-refresh.err</string>
</dict>
</plist>
